syntax:
template< class InputIt, class OutputIt >
OutputIt unique_copy( InputIt first, InputIt last, OutputIt d_first );

// With binary predicate:
template< class InputIt, class OutputIt, class BinaryPredicate >
OutputIt unique_copy( InputIt first, InputIt last, OutputIt d_first, BinaryPredicate p );

Implementation notes: 'InputIt' must be at least a forward iterator, and 'OutputIt' must support assignment. The compiler deduces these from the arguments, and the function skips consecutive duplicates by comparing *iter with the previously copied element. For the given example, std::ostream_iterator<string> is used as OutputIt, which internally buffers output and flushes on destruction, affecting performance in I/O-bound scenarios.

code_example:
#include <iostream>
#include <algorithm>
#include <vector>
#include <iterator>
#include <string>

int main() {
    std::vector<std::string> coll = {"apple", "banana", "apple", "orange"};
    std::unique_copy(coll.begin(), coll.end(),
                     std::ostream_iterator<std::string>(std::cout, "\n"));
    return 0;
}
